ar on a 16-bit machine: archive offsets as long, and member sizes up to 64K.

GNU ar (1990) kept archive offsets in int and passed them to fseek and lseek,
which take a long; in 16-bit K&R C the callee then reads a garbage high word,
and extraction failed with "Invalid argument". Offsets are now long, so an
archive can pass 64K. Member dates are parsed with atol (seconds since 1970
do not fit in 16 bits), and the copy loops, the size field and the verbose
listing treat a member's size as unsigned, so a member can be up to 64K.

